dsh-mini-remote — DISCLOSURE (dual-use content)
================================================================

This file declares the security-relevant ("dual-use") capabilities of this
package, as required by the npm Dual-Use Content Policy
(https://docs.npmjs.com/policies/dual-use). It describes what the software can
do and what it is legitimately for.


WHAT THIS PACKAGE IS
----------------------------------------------------------------
dsh-mini-remote is a host plugin for DeepSeek Harness (DSH), a desktop AI
harness. A user installs it on a computer they own and operate, and then uses
their own phone to reach that same computer.

It is not a service. There is no backend operated by the maintainer, no
telemetry, no analytics, and no code that executes on any machine other than
the one that installed the plugin and the device the owner paired to it.


CLASSIFICATION
----------------------------------------------------------------
This package is a tool "designed and documented explicitly", in the sense of the
Acceptable Content section of the npm Open Source Terms:

  "Content containing malicious computer code ... Tools designed and documented
   explicitly to assist in security research are acceptable, but exploits and
   malware that use the npm registry as a deployment or delivery vector are not."

To be explicit about which side of that line this package sits on:

- It is not malicious code. It does not self-propagate, does not hide its
  behaviour, does not hook or modify other processes, and does not persist
  beyond the plugin's own installation directory.
- It is not a deployment or delivery vector. The package does not fetch and run
  third-party code on anyone's machine. The only executable it ships is the FRP
  client described below, which the owner chooses to run for their own tunnel.
  The Android application referenced by the project is installed by the owner
  from a release the project publishes, not delivered through the registry.
- Every capability that could be mistaken for malicious behaviour is listed
  below by name, with its switch, its default, and how it is authorised.
- All security-relevant features are off by default. A fresh install listens on
  the loopback interface only and accepts no remote device until the machine's
  owner turns remote access on and pairs a device with a one-time code.


CAPABILITIES THAT AUTOMATED SCANNING MAY FLAG
----------------------------------------------------------------
1. Remote command execution.
   Once a phone is paired, the owner can send shell commands to the host
   computer and read their output. Used to run build, test, and git commands
   from the phone.

2. File read and write on the host.
   Restricted to folders the owner explicitly lists in the host settings. Paths
   outside that list are rejected by the host, not by the client.

3. Screen capture and input on a paired Android device.
   The companion Android application can show the phone screen and inject taps
   and text. This requires the device owner to grant the Android accessibility
   service; without that grant the capability is unavailable.

4. Tunnelling.
   - The package bundles one executable, lib/bin/frpc.exe, the official FRP
     client (github.com/fatedier/frp, v0.71.1, Windows amd64, built from the
     upstream release archive). Its Apache-2.0 licence ships beside it at
     lib/bin/frp-LICENSE, and lib/bin/README.md records the exact upstream
     version and file. It is spawned only when the owner enables the
     self-hosted relay, and the plugin accepts a path to a copy the owner
     installs themselves.
   - Cloudflare Tunnel (cloudflared) and a Tailscale-compatible node can be used
     for the same purpose when the owner turns them on. Those programs are
     downloaded from their official distributions, not bundled.
   - relay-server/install.sh documents how to deploy the optional relay on the
     owner's own server. It is not run by the package.

5. A local HTTP server and a WebSocket stream.
   Bound to the host, serving the phone page and the live session stream. Access
   requires either a password, a device credential issued at pairing time, or,
   from the loopback interface only, no credential at all.

6. Optional integrations.
   Feishu (Lark) notifications, and execution of scheduled tasks. Both are off
   unless the owner configures them.


HOW ACCESS IS AUTHORISED
----------------------------------------------------------------
- The host is closed to other devices until the owner turns on remote access.
- Pairing requires a one-time six-digit code that is displayed on the host.
- The owner chooses, per paired computer and per permission, whether the other
  side may write files, run commands, or manage folders.
- Every capability can be revoked from the host settings page at any time.
- Nothing is enabled by default: a fresh install answers only on loopback and
  accepts no remote device.


INTENDED LEGITIMATE USE
----------------------------------------------------------------
Using one's own phone to operate one's own development computer: reading the
session, replying to the agent, running commands, reviewing files, and getting
notified when a long task finishes. The same plugin also lets the owner drive a
second computer they own, and lets a paired phone be operated from the desktop.

The typical user is a developer working on a personal machine, often away from
the desk, who wants the same session on the phone without exposing the machine
to the public internet.


OUT OF SCOPE / NOT SUPPORTED
----------------------------------------------------------------
The package contains no persistence mechanism, no privilege escalation, no
credential harvesting, no obfuscated payload, and no mechanism for a third party
to obtain access without the owner generating a pairing code on the host. It
does not ship a keylogger, does not read files outside the authorised folders,
and does not communicate with any server operated by the maintainer.

It is also not a general-purpose tunnelling product sold as a service, and it
does not ship exploits. If a future version ever gains a capability that could
be read as an exploit or as a delivery mechanism for someone else's code, it
will be documented here before it ships.


